<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- Base application theme. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.Light.DarkActionBar">
        <!-- Customize your theme here. -->
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/colorPrimary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/colorPrimaryDark</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
        <item name="android:alertDialogTheme">@style/AppTheme.AlertDialog</item>
    </style>

    <!-- Explicit alert-dialog theme. The native <select> element's Android
         picker is a framework AlertDialog + ListView (confirmed via
         `uiautomator dump`: android:id/parentPanel, android:id/customPanel,
         android:id/text1 CheckedTextView rows). It was rendering the launch
         splash drawable (@drawable/splash, the full-bleed navy logo image)
         behind every option row.

         THE FIRST FIX WAS INCOMPLETE and the bug shipped in 1.3.2. It was
         re-measured on a Galaxy Z Fold5 on 2026-07-31 with the option rows
         at bounds [0,85][904,1485] and [0,1488][904,2277], so 1400px and
         789px tall against a normal ~150px. One option filled the screen,
         which made a 89-item category list unusable.

         Two things were missed the first time:

         1. AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch is what AndroidManifest actually
            declares on MainActivity, and it was the ONLY one of the three
            themes not declaring android:alertDialogTheme. So the dialog
            kept resolving the platform default. That is also why swapping
            AppTheme.NoActionBar's windowBackground to a diagnostic red had
            zero effect: the wrong theme was being edited.

         2. android:background is NOT a window attribute. Set as a theme
            item (which the launch theme does, for the pre-Android-12
            splash) it becomes the default background for any VIEW that
            does not set its own, including the dialog's CheckedTextView
            rows. A row backed by a full-bleed bitmap inherits that
            bitmap's intrinsic height, which is the single mechanism behind
            both the giant rows and the repeated logo. Neutralising
            android:background here is therefore the load-bearing line, not
            windowBackground.

         AND A THIRD THING WAS STILL MISSED, shipped in 1.3.5: the option
         rows came back to a normal height but the picker still painted
         solid brand navy. Neutralising android:background HERE only
         covers views built from the dialog's own themed context. The
         Chromium select popup builds its ListView from the ACTIVITY
         context, so that view resolves android:background from the
         ACTIVITY theme and never sees this @null. Proved on an API-34
         emulator: the picker's background sampled (18,26,42), the exact
         pixel of @drawable/splash and not the white set below, and
         setting a diagnostic red on AppTheme.NoActionBar (the theme
         MainActivity switches to) turned the picker red.

         RESOLVED AT SOURCE. AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch no longer sets
         android:background at all - it was only ever there on the false
         premise that it drove the pre-Android-12 splash - so nothing
         merges into the Activity theme and nothing reaches the popup's
         ListView. The @null restatement on AppTheme.NoActionBar that
         chased this bug through three releases is gone with it. The
         @null below is kept as a plain statement of intent for this
         dialog's own views, not as a defence against a drawable that no
         longer exists. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.AlertDialog" parent="android:Theme.DeviceDefault.Dialog.Alert">
        <!-- Dark on purpose. See picker_dialog_background.xml: the parent
             is the DARK DeviceDefault variant, so its list text is
             near-white, and the pages that open the picker are dark. The
             old @android:color/white was white-on-white and only looked
             survivable because the splash bitmap covered it. -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/picker_dialog_background</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>

        <!-- Pin the text colours instead of trusting the parent. On OneUI
             the DeviceDefault dialog follows the SYSTEM light/dark
             setting, so a phone in light mode would resolve dark text
             onto the dark surface above and the options would be
             unreadable. -->
        <item name="android:textColorPrimary">#f2f5fa</item>
        <item name="android:textColorSecondary">#b8c2d4</item>
        <item name="android:textColorAlertDialogListItem">#f2f5fa</item>
        <item name="android:colorAccent">@color/colorAccent</item>
        <item name="android:colorControlActivated">@color/colorAccent</item>

        <!-- Makes "tap outside to dismiss" physically possible. The
             framework floating-dialog default is windowMinWidthMinor
             95%, which on a 1080px-wide screen leaves a 27px margin,
             while Window.isOutOfBounds() only counts a touch as outside
             once it clears the decor by getScaledWindowTouchSlop(),
             which is 16dp, roughly 44px at that density. Every side tap
             therefore landed INSIDE the slop and the dialog never
             cancelled; with an 89-item list filling the height there was
             no vertical outside either, so Back was the only way out.
             Cancel-on-touch-outside was never disabled, it was simply
             unreachable: lowering this floor to 72% moved the dialog to
             [141,...][939,...] on that screen, a 141px strip either side,
             and the same tap then dismissed it.

             Width only, and a floor rather than windowFixedWidthMinor,
             which is a private attribute the app cannot set. A fixed
             HEIGHT would force a 3-option select to open as a giant
             half-empty sheet. Long labels do not defeat the floor: the
             Chromium row wraps to a second line instead of demanding a
             wider dialog (measured with 40-character category names). -->
        <item name="android:windowMinWidthMinor">72%</item>
        <item name="android:windowMinWidthMajor">55%</item>
    </style>

    <!-- Post-splash theme Capacitor's BridgeActivity switches to.
         Brand dark green (#121a2a) is the WINDOW background AND the
         status/navigation bar colours so there is never a white
         strip: pre-Android-15 the bars are coloured directly; on
         Android 15 colours: window background #121a2a (shows behind
         the bars) + white glyphs, so there's never a white strip.

         windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement=true: Android 15 (API 35)
         FORCES edge-to-edge for targetSdk 35, which makes the WebView
         draw under the status bar. The Android WebView reports
         env(safe-area-inset-top)=0, so the header rendered on top of
         the clock/battery ("header overlapping the notification
         bar"); StatusBar.setOverlaysWebView(false) is ignored under
         forced edge-to-edge (confirmed in logcat on an API-35
         emulator). This official opt-out restores normal insets so
         the OS reserves the status-bar strip and the header starts
         below it. Valid until we target SDK 36 / handle insets
         natively; harmless/ignored on pre-15. -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#121a2a</item>
        <!-- android:background is deliberately absent, and that is now the
             fix rather than a workaround. It used to be restated as @null
             here to neutralise the @drawable/splash that
             AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch set and that Activity.setTheme
             MERGED (Resources.Theme.applyStyle with force=true) into this
             theme for the Activity's whole life. The launch theme no longer
             sets android:background at all, so there is nothing to inherit
             and nothing to neutralise: the framework default (no view
             background) applies, and the window still paints #121a2a. -->
        <item name="android:alertDialogTheme">@style/AppTheme.AlertDialog</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">#121a2a</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">#121a2a</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceStatusBarContrast">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceNavigationBarContrast">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement">true</item>
    </style>


    <!-- Launch/splash theme.

         Both launch paths render the brand mark from the SAME two
         compat attributes below. These are the androidx
         core-splashscreen attrs (no android: prefix); the library maps
         them to the platform attrs on API 31+ and back-ports them
         below that.

         Android 12+ (API 31+): the OS centres
         `windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` on
         `windowSplashScreenBackground` and applies its own masking. It
         ignores android:windowBackground for the splash content. The
         mapping lives in the library's values-v31 Theme.SplashScreen.

         API 24-30: Theme.SplashScreen.Common (no values-v31 variant)
         sets android:windowBackground to
         @drawable/compat_splash_screen_no_icon_background. Because
         minSdk is 24, that drawable has exactly ONE config in the APK,
         res/drawable-v23, which is a layer-list of: the background
         colour, the icon centred at
         @dimen/splashscreen_icon_size_no_background = 288dp, then an
         oval STROKE 109dp wide on a 410dp box painted in the
         background colour. An Android shape stroke is centred on its
         path, so that ring covers everything outside a
         410 - 2x109 = 192dp circle. The library's own comment: "We
         mask the outer bounds of the icon like we do on Android 12."

         So the icon asset has to be built for that treatment: a 288dp
         transparent square with the artwork inside the centred 192dp
         circle. @drawable/splash_logo is exactly that - the brand mark
         (public/brand/icon-mark-1024.png, white column-T + gold growth
         chart) in a centred 132dp box, whose 186.7dp diagonal clears
         the 192dp circle with margin.

         DO NOT point this at @mipmap/ic_launcher, and do not leave it
         unset. Unset is what produced the earlier incident: the OS
         fell back to the full ADAPTIVE launcher icon, which carries
         its own ic_launcher_background layer, and then circle-masked
         it, so the splash showed a mismatched background disc instead
         of the clean mark on navy. splash_logo is foreground artwork
         only, with no background layer for a mask to expose.

         (@drawable/splash, the full-bleed navy plate, is NOT used by
         this theme. It belongs to the @capacitor/splash-screen plugin,
         which looks it up by name and shows it for launchShowDuration
         after this theme has handed over.) -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">#121a2a</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_logo</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
        <!-- REQUIRED. This is the theme AndroidManifest declares on
             MainActivity, so it is the one the native <select> picker
             resolves its dialog theme from. Without it the picker falls
             back to the platform default dialog theme, which follows the
             system light/dark setting, and the option labels go
             unreadable on the app's dark pages. -->
        <item name="android:alertDialogTheme">@style/AppTheme.AlertDialog</item>
        <!-- There is deliberately no android:background here. It used to
             carry @drawable/splash "for the pre-Android-12 splash", which
             was never true: android:background is a VIEW attribute and can
             never be a window background, and no view exists yet at launch.
             The pre-31 branded window comes from windowSplashScreenBackground
             via the library's android:windowBackground, as described above.

             What the line did do was leak. As a theme item it became the
             default background of every plain view, and Activity.setTheme
             MERGES rather than replaces, so it survived into
             AppTheme.NoActionBar for the Activity's whole life and the
             Chromium <select> popup built its ListView with a full-bleed
             bitmap background - 1400px tall option rows with the splash
             painted behind the text. Removing it ends that at source. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">#121a2a</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">#121a2a</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
